WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Equilibrium calculations stump a lot of students, and

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.360
it is rarely the algebra.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.640
It is a small trap early on: moles

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.000
where K needs concentrations, a coefficient missed in

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.400
the change row, a value that does not

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.720
start at zero, or a solid left in

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.200
K.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.280
Here are two HSC-style questions, and part two

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.240
has two more.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.800
Each hides at least one trap.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.680
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.520
it through one step at a time, and

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.200
look at the classic mistake.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.280
Question one.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.280
Iron two oxide, a solid, reacts with carbon

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.720
monoxide to make solid iron and carbon dioxide.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.280
At about 800 degrees, K is 0.50.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.760
A sealed 2.00 litre vessel holds an excess

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.680
of iron two oxide and 0.600 moles of

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.280
carbon monoxide, with no iron or carbon dioxide

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.000
at the start.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.000
What mass of iron is present at equilibrium?

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.360
Pause here to answer.

00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:15.246
Start with the expression.

00:01:15.527 --> 00:01:18.566
Iron two oxide and iron are solids, so

00:01:18.727 --> 00:01:20.086
they stay out of K.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:23.766
That leaves K equals carbon dioxide over carbon

00:01:23.847 --> 00:01:26.086
monoxide, with no powers at all.

00:01:26.647 --> 00:01:29.046
Now the I C E table: I for

00:01:29.127 --> 00:01:32.566
initial, C for change, E for equilibrium.

00:01:33.127 --> 00:01:35.846
The carbon monoxide is in moles, so divide

00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:40.886
first: 0.600 over 2.00 is

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:45.686
0.300 moles per litre, and carbon dioxide starts

00:01:45.727 --> 00:01:46.566
at zero.

00:01:46.887 --> 00:01:49.606
Nothing at equilibrium is measured, so the change

00:01:49.687 --> 00:01:54.646
is x: carbon monoxide ends at 0.300 minus

00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:57.446
x, and carbon dioxide at x.

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:03.386
So x over 0.300 minus x equals

00:02:03.547 --> 00:02:05.386
0.50.

00:02:05.507 --> 00:02:07.946
K is not tiny, so do not drop

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:08.746
the x.

00:02:08.907 --> 00:02:11.146
You do not need to: this equation is

00:02:11.227 --> 00:02:12.466
already simple.

00:02:12.747 --> 00:02:17.146
Multiply both sides by the bottom, 0.300 minus

00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:23.466
x: x equals 0.150 minus 0.50 x.

00:02:23.707 --> 00:02:27.866
Add 0.50 x to both sides, so 1.50

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:31.306
x is 0.150.

00:02:31.387 --> 00:02:35.226
Then divide both sides by 1.50, and x

00:02:35.307 --> 00:02:37.706
is 0.100.

00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:42.666
Check: 0.100 over 0.200 is

00:02:42.747 --> 00:02:44.266
0.50.

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:47.506
But the question asks for a mass of

00:02:47.547 --> 00:02:48.026
iron.

00:02:48.427 --> 00:02:51.066
The equation makes one iron for every carbon

00:02:51.147 --> 00:02:54.426
dioxide, so the moles of iron formed equal

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:56.506
the moles of carbon dioxide formed.

00:02:56.987 --> 00:03:01.866
That is 0.100 moles per litre, times 2.00

00:03:01.987 --> 00:03:06.546
litres, which is 0.200 moles.

00:03:06.827 --> 00:03:10.906
Times 55.85 grams per mole, that is 11.2

00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:12.666
grams of iron.

00:03:13.227 --> 00:03:16.186
The classic mistake is stopping at the concentration,

00:03:16.307 --> 00:03:19.866
or forgetting the volume and getting 5.6 grams.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:21.946
Question two.

00:03:22.587 --> 00:03:26.666
Carbon monoxide and hydrogen make methanol gas: C

00:03:26.747 --> 00:03:29.386
O plus two H two makes methanol.

00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:34.266
A 2.00 litre vessel at about 230 degrees

00:03:34.587 --> 00:03:39.786
starts with 0.200 moles of carbon monoxide, 0.400

00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:44.426
moles of hydrogen and 0.400 moles

00:03:44.507 --> 00:03:45.786
of methanol.

00:03:45.867 --> 00:03:50.626
At equilibrium, it holds 0.320 moles of methanol.

00:03:50.907 --> 00:03:52.186
Calculate K.

00:03:52.507 --> 00:03:53.786
Pause here to answer.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.480
Everything here is in moles, so first divide

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.640
by 2.00.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.120
The initial row is 0.100 for carbon monoxide,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.760
0.200 for hydrogen and 0.200 for

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:19.320
methanol.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:25.400
The measured methanol, 0.320 moles, is 0.160 moles

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.880
per litre, and it goes in the equilibrium

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:28.480
row.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.880
Change is equilibrium minus initial: 0.160 minus

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:39.160
0.200 is minus 0.0400.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.720
Methanol went down, so this mixture ran backwards.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.600
You did not have to guess that: the

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.440
subtraction gave you the sign.

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:49.186
Now the coefficients.

00:04:49.507 --> 00:04:52.706
Each methanol that splits gives one carbon monoxide

00:04:52.867 --> 00:04:56.706
and two hydrogen, so carbon monoxide rises by

00:04:56.867 --> 00:05:03.986
0.0400 and hydrogen rises by 0.0800.

00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:07.186
Add each change to its start: carbon monoxide

00:05:07.587 --> 00:05:12.946
0.140, hydrogen 0.280.

00:05:13.147 --> 00:05:16.546
K is methanol over carbon monoxide times hydrogen

00:05:16.627 --> 00:05:17.666
squared.

00:05:17.787 --> 00:05:22.706
0.280 squared is 0.0784,

00:05:22.787 --> 00:05:29.106
and times 0.140 that is 0.01098.

00:05:29.187 --> 00:05:31.426
Keep the extra figures until the end.

00:05:31.547 --> 00:05:36.306
0.160 over 0.01098 gives

00:05:36.707 --> 00:05:38.946
K is 14.6.

00:05:40.167 --> 00:05:42.806
The classic mistake here is writing the measured

00:05:42.887 --> 00:05:46.526
0.160 straight into the change row.

00:05:46.807 --> 00:05:48.886
It is not a change, it is where

00:05:48.967 --> 00:05:50.406
methanol ended up.

00:05:50.727 --> 00:05:52.806
Put it in the equilibrium row, and let

00:05:52.927 --> 00:05:56.166
equilibrium minus initial give you the change, sign

00:05:56.247 --> 00:05:56.886
and all.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.700
So here is what to look for.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.980
Moles and a volume: divide first.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.580
One equilibrium value: it goes in the equilibrium

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.020
row, and equilibrium minus initial gives the change

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.060
and its sign.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.180
A coefficient: the change row follows it.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.300
A solid, or water as the solvent: leave

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:18.340
it out of K.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.620
Asked for grams: convert back at the end.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.340
Next: part two, with a K for the

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.140
other equation, a tiny K, and a hidden

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.260
perfect square.

00:06:29.467 --> 00:06:30.906
Here are the key notes.

00:06:31.147 --> 00:06:33.066
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:33.147 --> 00:06:33.946
to copy them.
